 "asked_at": "2026-10-17T12:59+08:00",
 "answer": "They may be showing **different measures**, not contradicting each other.\n\nIf you’re in Singapore, the official **PSI is a 24-hour measure** that combines several pollutants. Some apps instead show a **short-term PM2.5 reading** or use another country’s AQI scale, so a brief pollution spike can look “unhealthy” there while the 24-hour PSI remains “moderate.” ([nea.gov.sg](https://www.nea.gov.sg/media/readers-letters/index/pm-2-5-for-immediate-activities-psi-forecast-for-next-day?utm_source=openai))\n\nThe labels also don’t line up exactly: Singapore’s PSI is **Moderate at 51–100** and **Unhealthy at 101–200**; the U.S. AQI calls **101–150 “Unhealthy for Sensitive Groups.”** ([moh.gov.sg](https://www.moh.gov.sg/others/haze/?utm_source=openai))\n\nCheck whether both displays use the same **location, pollutant, averaging period, and index**. If you share the app names and readings, I can help compare them.",
